# SYNOPSIS

```usage
distd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`distd` serves the content store and the releases in it as `/dist`.
A release's base tree is served read-only, and every byte read from it is first checked against the tree's hash.
On an installed system it also stages a release in one of the ESP's boot slots, and rolls back to the slot before.

svcd(8) starts it from a manifest (see svc(6)) whose `post=` it serves, usually `post=dist`, as `/srv/dist`.
It takes no arguments.
Its namespace, from the manifest's `mount=` lines, holds:

: `/n`
  The store: the system volume's `store` branch, served by fsd(4).
  Objects are files `b2/`<xx>`/`<hash>, named by the 64 hex digits of their BLAKE2b-256 hash, <xx> the first two; release records are `records/*.ndb`.
: `/tmp`
  The ESP, served by dosfs(4), for the boot slots.
: `/adm`
  fsd's `adm` branch, whose `ctl` snapshots and rolls back `/cfg`.

and, with the manifest's `cmdline`, the kernel's command line, whose `vx.slot=`<x> says which slot booted.
Only `/n` is needed to serve the store; `/tmp`, `/adm` and `vx.slot=` are for the slots, and an installed system's manifest runs `distd` as `adm` with all of them.

At its start, and on `rescan`, `distd` reads every record in `/n/records`.
A release is one whose record has a `release=`<n> and a `set=base` record for its own architecture with a `tree=` hash (see release(6)); others are skipped.
It keeps 16 at most, and skips a record over 16 KiB.

@node=verified title="Verified reads"

The store's formats are store(6)'s: a file is an index naming its 64 KiB blocks, and a directory is ndb text naming its entries, each object named by its hash.
`distd` reads a release's tree from the top: each directory is checked against its hash before an entry of it is used, and each file's index against the file's hash, and the size the index gives against the size its directory gives.
A read of a file then checks each block it touches against the index before a byte of it is returned.

A block or index that does not match is refused: the read fails with an I/O error, nothing of the block is returned, and `distd` says the file's path in the tree on the console.
A directory that does not match is refused with an I/O error too.
Objects that matched are kept in a cache of 24, and are not checked again while they stay there.

`/dist/store` is not checked: it gives the objects as the store holds them, for a peer, which checks them itself.

@node=slots title="Slots"

The ESP holds three boot slots, `\EFI\vectra\a`, `b` and `c`, each one release's kernel, root-task modules and bootfs, and a table of them, `\EFI\vectra\slots.ndb` (see slots(6)), which says what each slot holds, which slot boots, and which booted before.
Limine's configuration, `\boot\limine\limine.conf`, is made from the table alone: an entry for each slot in use, each file with its BLAKE2b-512 hash, which Limine checks, and the slot that boots as the default.
install(8) writes slot a and both files; `distd` changes them.

`apply` <n> stages release <n>:

- its whole tree is checked: every directory, index and block present and matching its hash;
- `/cfg` is snapshotted as `cfg@apply-`<n>, through `/adm/ctl`, replacing a snapshot of that name from an earlier apply;
- the release's `boot/vx/kernel.elf`, `svcd`, `ktest` and `bootfs.tar` are written to the slot that neither boots nor booted before, each checked block by block as it is read from the store, then read back from the ESP and compared with its hash;
- the table is rewritten with the slot holding release <n> and booting, the slot that booted before as the previous, and Limine's configuration made again from it.

`rollback` makes the previous slot the one that boots and the one that boots the previous, rewrites the table and Limine's configuration, then rolls `/cfg` back to `cfg@apply-`<n>, <n> the release of the slot being left: `/cfg` as it was before that release was applied.

Both take effect at the next boot.
Neither reboots, and the running system's base stays the bootfs it booted with: `distd` serves the store, it does not switch to it.

